BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Exhibition on View | Sites of Remaking: Port Cities and Our Presen
 t — Providence
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-04:00":20261019T100000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-04:00":20261019T170000
DTSTAMP:20261019T222410Z
UID:ef0ff51078c7a53ec9e8445d0a29@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\, Open to the Public\,Providence\,United States
DESCRIPTION:This exhibition\, Sites of Remaking: Port Cities and Our Prese
 nt\, invites visitors to reconsider and recontextualize port cities\, cent
 ering Black and Indigenous experiences within the global history of the Tr
 ansatlantic Slave Trade and its present-day legacies. It explores themes o
 f freedom-making\, resistance\, place-making and the legacies of slavery t
 hrough works by three Rhode Island-based multi-disciplinary artists: Jazzm
 en Lee-Johnson\, Kia Lenise and Spencer Evans. Curated by Ivie Orobaton\, 
 A.M. Candidate in Public Humanities. The exhibition is an artistic compani
 on to the April 23–24 conference\, Reconsidering Port Cities: Critical C
 ommemoration of Slavery\, and Transatlantic Legacies \, ho\nCategory:  Ope
 n to the Public\nOriginal listing: https://events.brown.edu/cssj/event/334
 394-exhibition-on-view-sites-of-remaking-port-cities
LOCATION:Ruth J. Simmons Center for the Study of Slavery & Justice\, 94 Wa
 terman St.\, Providence\, RI 02906\, Ruth J. Simmons Center for the Study 
 of Slavery & Justice\, 94 Waterman St.\, Providence\, RI 02906
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://events.brown.edu/cssj/event/334394-exhibition-on-view-sites-of
 -remaking-port-cities
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:brown-arts
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Book Launch: The Routledge Handbook of Ideology Analysis — Washi
 ngton\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-04:00":20261019T100000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-04:00":20261019T110000
DTSTAMP:20261019T222410Z
UID:da2025e0a14d14d7681f71b461fa@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Academic\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:Join us to discuss The Routledge Handbook of Ideology Analysis
  with its editors: Juliette Faure\, Mathew Humphrey\, and David Laycock. T
 his handbook offers a key interdisciplinary reference to advance ideology 
 analysis and takes up many themes\, including how ideological activity rel
 ates to institutions\, organizations\, and cultural practices\; how ideolo
 gy is created and disseminated through political parties\, social movement
 s\, think tanks\, on social media\, and elsewhere\; and more.\nCategory: A
 cademic\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/book-launch-the-
 routledge-handbook-of-ideology-analysis
LOCATION:George Washington University\, Washington\, DC
STATUS:CONFIRMED
