 ion\, and invites people into participatory experience. Three making stati
 ons extend the artists’ inquiries into shared practice. Visitors are inv
 ited to create mirror shields in solidarity with water protectors\, make c
 lay beads representing the wild bison population in America as part of Cen
 sus\, or respond to banned books\, suppressed texts\, and contested images
  through collage\, image-making\, and other forms of reinterpretation. Tog
 ether\, the exhibitions foreground artistic practice as a form of p\nCateg
